<div style="width: 100%; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Healing Plants of Panama – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present basic information about personal and community topics. Interactive: Exchange simple ideas in group discussions about current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present basic information on a plant's uses (e.g., "Guanabana (soursop) is used to treat infections.").
            Can exchange information about medicinal plants used in Panamanian home remedies (e.g., "My grandfather Bill uses ginger tea for colds. What does your family use?").
            Can ask and answer questions about plant benefits (e.g., "What is sabila (aloe vera) used for?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and divides them into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a picture of a common Panamanian medicinal plant (e.g., sábila, ginger, lemongrass, mint leaves). Students discuss in their groups for two minutes what they know about the plant, focusing on its uses. The teacher prompts them with: "What is this plant? What is it used for? Does your family use it?" Each group then shares one idea with the class. This activates prior knowledge from previous lessons and gets students speaking immediately.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words from the previous lesson, especially compound nouns like "herbal garden," "mint leaves," and "soursop." The teacher demonstrates the target grammar: "Sábila *is used to* heal burns." and "You *should* apply it for burns." The teacher uses gestures and realia (if available) or pictures to clarify the meaning of verbs like "treat," "soothe," "apply," and "heal." The teacher also models syllable division for words like "treatment" (treat-ment) and "therapeutic" (ther-a-peu-tic) by clapping or finger-snapping.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled language and concepts.
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I have a burn, *what* plant should I use?" (Expected answer: "Sábila / Aloe vera.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we say 'is used to heal,' are we talking about the past or generally?" (Expected answer: "Generally, for its purpose.")</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short dialogue between two friends discussing minor ailments and traditional home remedies. The dialogue features the theme "Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns" and other healing plants of Panama. The register is informal, and the format is a conversation. Example lines include: "My little sister has a burn. What should I do?" "You should use sábila. It is used to soothe burns." "And what about a cold?" "Ginger tea is good for colds. My grandmother boils the root to prepare it."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The teacher focuses on the sociolinguistic competence of presenting and exchanging simple health-related advice and remedies in discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue, which is presented either by the teacher, via audio, or by two confident students.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the friends talking about?" (Expected answer: "Home remedies / Medicinal plants and their uses.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students indicate if sentences about the dialogue are true or false (e.g., "Sábila is used for infections." - False).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match ailments (e.g., burn, cold) with the correct plant remedy and its use mentioned in the dialogue (e.g., "Burn -> Sábila -> used to soothe").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using the infinitive of purpose ("used to + infinitive verb"), "should/shouldn't" for advice, and the present simple for facts. They use vocabulary related to plants, remedies, and ailments (plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves, treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare, useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic, for, with, traditionally, safely).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings on the board or flashcards. Students complete them orally, focusing on the target grammar. E.g., "Mint leaves _______ (relieve headaches)." (Students say: "Mint leaves *are used to relieve* headaches.") "You _________ (apply) sábila for burns." (Students say: "You *should apply* sábila for burns.") This is done in open class and then in pairs. The teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of "treatment" and "therapeutic."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice - Picture Prompt & Advice:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher shows flashcards with pictures of different plants and an ailment (e.g., 'ginger' + 'cold', 'soursop' + 'infections', 'lemongrass' + 'fever'). Partner A asks: "What is [plant name] used for?" or "I have [ailment]. What should I do?" Partner B responds using "It is used to..." or "You should use..." and the appropriate vocabulary. The teacher ensures correct stress on compound nouns like "ginger tea" and encourages the use of prepositions "for" and "with."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Family Remedies Exchange":</b> Students receive a simple card with a plant and a family member (e.g., "Grandfather Bill uses ginger tea for colds," "My aunt uses mint leaves for headaches"). In pairs, they exchange information about these home remedies and ask follow-up questions. E.g., Student A: "My grandfather Bill uses ginger tea for colds. What does your family use?" Student B: "My mother uses sábila for burns. It's a natural remedy." They practice asking and answering questions like "What does your family use?" and "What is it used for?" The teacher encourages natural interaction and correct pronunciation.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Fluency focus and communicative task completion)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play: "Herbal Remedies Consultant". Students work in groups of three. One student acts as the "Consultant," another as a "Client" describing a health issue, and the third as an "Observer/Note-taker." Each group receives a scenario card (e.g., "Client has a skin irritation," "Client feels tired and has a headache," "Client has a minor cut").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Client" describes their problem. The "Consultant" asks questions and then recommends a Panamanian medicinal plant, explaining its use and giving advice using the target structures (e.g., "You should use sábila. It is a very useful plant and is used to treat skin irritations and heal minor cuts."). They also use vocabulary such as "natural," "therapeutic," "remedy," "application."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Observer/Note-taker" ensures correct grammar and pronunciation (especially syllable division in words like "application" or "therapeutic") and notes down the plant recommended and the advice given.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students switch roles after each scenario. Each group performs 2-3 scenarios. The teacher walks around, listening, providing support, and noting examples of good language use and common errors.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity directly connects to the theme "Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns" and the scenario "The Healing Plants of Panama," focusing on exchanging simple health-related advice and remedies, and presenting information productively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment and self-assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play and a quick peer assessment using simple criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Herbal Remedies Consultant" role-play in their groups. The teacher observes and uses a checklist to assess each student's speaking ability.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (of words like "treatment," "therapeutic," compound nouns).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "used to + infinitive" for purpose.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "should/shouldn't" for giving advice.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (plant, remedy, heal, soothe, etc.).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of information presented.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and interactive exchange with peers.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Name one medicinal plant and what it is used for.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Give one piece of advice about using a plant for a health issue (use 'should').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How confident do you feel talking about Panamanian healing plants now? (Circle: Very / A little / Not yet)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most interesting fact you learned about Panamanian healing plants today?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about giving advice in English?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this speaking lesson? The role-play encouraged authentic communication and use of target structures. Students showed good recall of vocabulary. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with fluency and organizing their thoughts. Providing more sentence starters or a quick review of verb conjugations for present simple might be helpful. I noticed some difficulty distinguishing between "treat" and "heal." I will reinforce this in the next lesson. The pronunciation of multi-syllable words and compound nouns will also need continuous practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about healing plants. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write short paragraphs or instructions about how to use these remedies, consolidating the vocabulary and grammatical structures learned today through a different skill.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>